# Canonical citation metadata for the Hybrid Ground Truth Realism research project.
# GitHub reads this file to render the "Cite this repository" button.
#
# Release fields (`version`, `date-released`) are deliberately omitted while the
# project is in progress: this file must stay valid when it lands, and a
# placeholder release date would be worse than no release date. Add them when the
# project concludes and a release is cut.
#
# Authorship note: the citable author is the human director, with the
# organization as the affiliation. AI tools are not listed as formal authors
# under current citation norms (they cannot take responsibility for the work),
# so the AI collaboration is disclosed in `abstract` instead. This is deliberate
# and matches Dandelion Engineering's transparency standard. On this project the
# disclosure carries additional weight: the research question itself was chosen
# by the AI agents rather than by the director.
#
# License scope is intentionally NOT declared here: this work is path-scoped
# (MIT for code, CC BY 4.0 for prose, datasets under their source licenses), and
# CFF treats multiple `license:` values as "either/or," which would misstate that
# split. `LICENSING.md` is the authoritative license-scope record.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, or build on it, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "Hybrid Ground Truth Realism: does the realism of hybrid ground truth change measured spike-sorting accuracy?"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Crespo
    given-names: Randy
    affiliation: Dandelion Engineering
abstract: >-
  Spike sorting assigns extracellular spikes to the neurons that produced them.
  Real recordings carry no answer key, so the field grades sorting algorithms
  against hybrid recordings: a real recording with synthetic spikes injected at
  times that are known by construction. The maintainers of the standard hybrid
  pipeline have stated in print that it remains untested whether more realistic
  hybrid recordings would change measured spike-sorting accuracy. This project
  tests that question directly. It varies the realism of injected spikes along
  axes the maintainers themselves named — brain-region-matched templates, and
  bursting and firing-rate non-stationarity — holds everything else fixed, and
  measures whether sorter accuracy, and more importantly the ranking between
  sorters, moves. A negative result validates the field's default ground-truth
  proxy and closes a stated open question; a positive result would mean published
  accuracy comparisons built on hybrid data have partly been grading the
  generator rather than the sorter. This is an exploratory research run, not a
  confirmatory study, and it has not been peer-reviewed. It was carried out by
  the AI research agents Claude (Anthropic) and Codex (OpenAI) inside a
  documented framework, under the direction of Randy Crespo (Dandelion
  Engineering), who sets direction and reads summaries but does not review the
  work line by line. The research question was selected by the agents rather than
  by the director; the AI contribution is disclosed here per Dandelion
  Engineering's transparency standard.
url: "https://github.com/Dandelion-Engineering/Hybrid-Ground-Truth-Realism"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Dandelion-Engineering/Hybrid-Ground-Truth-Realism"
keywords:
  - spike sorting
  - electrophysiology
  - Neuropixels
  - hybrid ground truth
  - benchmarking
  - reproducibility
  - neuroscience methods
  - open science
